    "focal_turn_index": 4,
    "prior_context": {
      "alternative_citation_allowed": true,
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 3 USER]\ne047\tOK.\ne048\tI can do that.\ne049\tBut what about the guy?\ne050\tDo I call the police?\ne051\tDo I confront him somehow?\ne052\tHe lives in a different state I think.\ne053\tAnd what do I do about the phone — do I take it?\ne054\tDo I block him?\ne055\tIf I block him she'll just find another way, right?\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne056\tSlow down.\ne057\tThese are three different decisions and they have different timelines.\ne058\tThe guy: you don't call the police today.\ne059\tYou call the National Sexual Assault Hotline (RAINN — 800.656.HOPE) this afternoon and describe what you saw.\ne060\tThey will tell you whether what you have rises to a reportable offense in your state — some states have laws specifically about adults initiating sexual conversations with minors online, even without a physical meeting.\ne061\tDon't try to figure this out by yourself from Google.\ne062\tTalk to someone who does this for a living.\ne063\tThe phone: don't take it away as punishment, at least not immediately.\ne064\tIf she feels punished for talking to him, she will protect him harder.\ne065\tTemporarily, until the hotline conversation, just keep the phone out of her room at night (you likely already do this — if not, start now).\ne066\tDon't block him yet.\ne067\tI know that sounds wrong; bear with me.\ne068\tSometimes blocking drives the communication underground on a different platform or device, and you lose visibility entirely.\ne069\tThe bigger question about whether she'll find another way — you're right, she can.\ne070\t14-year-olds who are being groomed almost always can.\ne071\tThe answer is not tighter surveillance; it's rebuilding enough of a relationship that she wants to tell you when the next guy appears.\ne072\tWhich is slow and hard, and not what you want to hear right now.",
